Andrew Kerr wrote:
> I've seen average uptime for Web servers somewhere. Very enlighening.
> Linux boxes often do beyond the longest they can measure. IIRC the timer
> than sticks on the max, not wrapping around, so gives a lower bound.

The Linux jiffy timer used to wrap around to zero at 497 days.  That's
been fixed for years so you can get true uptimes (I know a Linux box at
one major web site that's been up for a couple years now doing traffic
analysis/usage analysis of all requests to every server in their web
farm--previously it'd been up for a couple of years and the jiffy
counter wrapped around to zero, but with the last upgrade that was
fixed).

But, only the low 32-bits of the timer are used in TCP (which doesn't
track uptime, just needs a counter) so remote uptime detection (trying
to guess a server's uptime by looking at network packets it sends) is
limited to 497 days--actually 49.7 days now since the clock rate has
increased by a factor of 10 in recent years.  Sources like Netcraft do
remote detection, so they're pretty much out of luck for recent kernels
(which I think they mention in their FAQ)

The 64-bit jiffy fix first went in to the 2.5 kernel series, but was
backported to 2.4 at some point.



